# AdMob IDs. Copy this file to `.env` — it is ready to build as it stands.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# The values below are GOOGLE'S PUBLIC TEST IDs, documented and account-free.
# They serve house ads marked "Test Ad" and earn nothing, which is the correct
# default: a fresh clone builds and runs without an AdMob account, and a build
# made by someone who never read this file cannot bill impressions to anyone.
#
# **This file is the only place an ID lives.** `app.config.ts` reads the App IDs
# from here and has no fallback of its own — a missing `.env` fails the build
# rather than quietly substituting something. A default in two places is a
# default that disagrees with itself eventually, and the one time it matters is
# a release that looks fine and earns nothing.
#
# These are NOT secrets. Anyone can unzip an APK and read them. They live
# outside the repo because they are account-specific, not because they are
# sensitive: the company's AdMob account should be able to change them without
# editing TypeScript.
#
# Read at BUILD time, not at run time. Changing any of them means a rebuild, and
# changing an APP_ID also means `npm run prebuild` — those two are written into
# the native manifest before any JavaScript runs.

# One per platform, always. The `~` is what marks an App ID.
#
# Replace both with the real ones from the AdMob console at launch. A blank
# value is not the same as a missing one and is worse: it reaches the native
# manifest as an empty string, and the SDK reads that before any JavaScript
# runs, so the app crashes at launch rather than failing an ad request.
ADMOB_ANDROID_APP_ID=ca-app-pub-3940256099942544~3347511713
ADMOB_IOS_APP_ID=ca-app-pub-3940256099942544~1458002511

# The ad units. Note the `/` — that is what marks a unit, against the `~` on an
# App ID above.
#
# Unset here on purpose, unlike the App IDs. `src/ads/units.ts` falls back to
# the SDK's own test units, so there is nothing to copy and no ID to keep in
# step; fill these in only when the real ones exist.
#
# **One per platform, and one per format.** An AdMob unit belongs to a single
# app entry, so an Android unit means nothing to the iOS app; and a unit has a
# format, so asking for an interstitial against a rewarded unit is answered with
# no-fill rather than an error. Four names, no sharing.
#
# This block used to read `EXPO_PUBLIC_ADMOB_REWARDED=`, which nothing has ever
# read — `units.ts` wants the two platform-suffixed names below. Anyone
# following this file would have filled in a variable that does nothing and
# served test ads forever, which is the exact failure the notes above promise
# this file prevents.
#
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_` is required: it is what tells Expo to inline the value into the
# JS bundle. A name without that prefix is invisible to the app at run time.
EXPO_PUBLIC_ADMOB_REWARDED_ANDROID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_ADMOB_REWARDED_IOS=
EXPO_PUBLIC_ADMOB_INTERSTITIAL_ANDROID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_ADMOB_INTERSTITIAL_IOS=

# The switch that lets live IDs be used at all. Anything other than `true` keeps
# the build on test ads however many real IDs are set above.
#
# Separate from the IDs on purpose. A release APK sideloaded for testing is not
# a development build, and this project is tested exactly that way — without
# this, every one of those impressions would be live traffic against the
# account. Set it to `true` only for a build that is genuinely going to a store.
EXPO_PUBLIC_ADMOB_LIVE=false

# Pretend this device is in the EEA, so Google's consent form appears.
#
# Whether a form is needed is Google's decision, made server-side from the
# request's IP — so outside Europe it never shows and the sequence the app is
# arranged around (consent form, then Apple's tracking prompt, then the SDK)
# cannot be seen in development. Set this to `1` and relaunch to run it here.
#
# Ignored outside a development build. `__DEV__` gates it as well as this
# variable, because `EXPO_PUBLIC_` names are inlined at build time and one left
# set in a shell would otherwise ship a store build showing every player on
# earth a GDPR form.
#
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_` values are inlined into the bundle at build time, so changing
# this does nothing until Metro is restarted with its cache cleared:
#
#   npx expo start --dev-client --clear
#
# No native rebuild is needed — it is a JavaScript change. While it is on, the
# consent state is reset on every launch and the result is logged as
# `[ads] consent <status> canRequestAds <bool>`, so the form appears every time
# rather than once.
#
# TURN IT BACK OFF BEFORE TESTING ANYTHING ELSE ABOUT ADS. Consent is required
# in the EEA, so until a consent message is published in the AdMob console it
# cannot be obtained — the SDK sets `canRequestAds` false and serves nothing at
# all, test units included. The symptom is "No ad available right now" on a
# screen with nothing wrong with it.
